[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Custom Directives Lifecycle and Implementation in Vue.js"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Vue.js provides a robust mechanism for reusing low-level DOM access through custom directives. While components remain the primary abstraction, custom directives are ideal for directly manipulating underlying DOM elements. This tutorial explores the complete directive lifecycle hooks and demonstrates how to implement a fully functional custom directive."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Directive Lifecycle Hooks"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A custom directive object can define several lifecycle functions (also known as hooks), which are called at specific stages of the element's lifecycle. These hooks receive different arguments such as el, binding, vnode, and prevVnode."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"created: Called before the bound element's attributes or event listeners are applied. Useful for initialization logic."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"beforeMount: Called right before the element is inserted into the DOM."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"mounted: Called when the bound element's parent component and all its children are mounted. This is the best hook for direct DOM manipulation."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"beforeUpdate: Called before the containing component's VNode is updated."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"updated: Called after the containing component's VNode AND the VNodes of its children have updated."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"beforeUnmount: Called before the bound element's parent component is unmounted."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"unmounted: Called when the bound element's parent component is unmounted and the element is removed from the DOM."},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementing a Click-Outside Directive"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"To illustrate the lifecycle hooks in practice, let us implement a v-click-outside directive. This directive detects clicks outside a specified element, which is commonly used for closing dropdowns or modals."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"const clickOutside = {
  mounted(el, binding) {
    el.__vueClickOutside__ = (event) => {
      if (!(el === event.target || el.contains(event.target))) {
        binding.value(event);
      }
    };
    document.addEventListener('click', el.__vueClickOutside__);
  },
  unmounted(el) {
    document.removeEventListener('click', el.__vueClickOutside__);
    delete el.__vueClickOutside__;
  }
};"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Registering and Using the Directive"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"Custom directives can be registered globally using app.directive or locally within a single component using the directives options object. Below is an example of local registration inside a Vue 3 SFC script setup block."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"<script setup>
import { ref } from 'vue';

const isOpen = ref(false);

const vClickOutside = {
  mounted(el, binding) {
    el.clickOutsideEvent = function(event) {
      if (!(el === event.target || el.contains(event.target))) {
        binding.value(event);
      }
    };
    document.addEventListener('click', el.clickOutsideEvent);
  },
  unmounted(el) {
    document.removeEventListener('click', el.clickOutsideEvent);
  }
};

const closeDropdown = () => {
  isOpen.value = false;
};
</script>"},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Template Usage"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"Once registered, you can apply the directive to any element in the template using the v- prefix followed by the camelCased or kebab-cased directive name."},{"order":20,"type":"code","text":"<template>
  <div class="dropdown-container" v-click-outside="closeDropdown">
    <button @click="isOpen = !isOpen">Toggle Dropdown</button>
    <div v-if="isOpen" class="dropdown-menu">
      <p>Dropdown content goes here.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</template>"},{"order":21,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices"},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always clean up event listeners or timers in the unmounted hook to prevent memory leaks."},{"order":23,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Favor component composition over custom directives when dealing with complex reactive state and template structures."},{"order":24,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use binding arguments and modifiers (e.g., v-example:arg.modifier) to pass dynamic configurations into your directive handlers efficiently."}]
